WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.263
Welcome to Part 3 of the deeper dive on
attribute-driven migration to Entra ID.

2
00:00:05.263 --> 00:00:11.040
Parts 1 and 2 built the foundation,
Gates 0 to 6: the tree read, the schema,

3
00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:16.513
the proof, sync, the group library,
the personas and the PIV binding.

4
00:00:16.513 --> 00:00:17.974
This part is the payoff.

5
00:00:17.974 --> 00:00:20.988
Gate 7 proves one mover across the tracks.

6
00:00:20.988 --> 00:00:27.013
Gate 8 cuts over by attribute query and
retires the organizational unit (OU) tree.

7
00:00:27.013 --> 00:00:30.300
Gate 9 keeps the attributes true for good.

8
00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:33.716
You'll see the validation kit's
commands, the files it reads,

9
00:00:33.716 --> 00:00:37.488
what a failing check looks like,
and the rollback each gate gives.

10
00:00:37.488 --> 00:00:40.206
The kit is read-only and fixture-tested,

11
00:00:40.206 --> 00:00:44.625
not proven against a live tenant,
so run it in a test tenant first.

12
00:00:44.625 --> 00:00:46.810
Design guidance is labeled.

13
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:54.223
Here are the three gates on one page,
so you know where this is going.

14
00:00:54.223 --> 00:00:59.822
Gate 7 is done when one personnel action
changes access only through the attributes.

15
00:00:59.822 --> 00:01:03.234
Gate 8 is done when every
population has cut over

16
00:01:03.234 --> 00:01:06.647
by query and nothing reads
OU placement any more.

17
00:01:06.647 --> 00:01:10.970
Gate 9 is done when drift scans
run, findings close at the source,

18
00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.547
and the nightly plan has nothing to do.

19
00:01:13.547 --> 00:01:15.498
The CISO owns all three.

20
00:01:15.498 --> 00:01:19.273
Watch the schedule, because this
is where the critical path lives.

21
00:01:19.273 --> 00:01:22.497
From the program's G3 in
week 36 there's no float:

22
00:01:22.497 --> 00:01:28.184
the transitional sync rules come out, the
tree freezes after the OU groups retire,

23
00:01:28.184 --> 00:01:31.834
and the last drift cycle
closes both gates in week 40.

24
00:01:31.834 --> 00:01:37.390
Our running example is the same budget
analyst, keyed by an existing enterprise ID.

25
00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:46.752
Gate 7 is the test of the whole model, and
it runs before any population cuts over.

26
00:01:46.752 --> 00:01:48.890
The setup takes three steps.

27
00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:53.854
Pick a pilot user who sponsors a non-person
identity and has a managed laptop,

28
00:01:53.854 --> 00:01:56.690
because one test then
reaches three tracks.

29
00:01:56.690 --> 00:02:00.227
Record the starting state:
groups, unit, persona,

30
00:02:00.227 --> 00:02:04.490
access packages, the device's
groups and the sponsorship.

31
00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:07.162
Then take a baseline
with the validation kit,

32
00:02:07.162 --> 00:02:10.990
running the HR and dynamic-group
checks against an input folder.

33
00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:13.884
Three files matter: the
owned exceptions list,

34
00:02:13.884 --> 00:02:19.215
the HR export of counts per organization
code, and the previous membership snapshot.

35
00:02:19.215 --> 00:02:23.740
Each run writes its own snapshot,
which becomes the next run's input.

36
00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:27.042
Finally, HR records the
move as a personnel action,

37
00:02:27.042 --> 00:02:30.828
a reassignment from the ledger
code to accounts payable.

38
00:02:30.828 --> 00:02:32.700
Nobody opens a ticket.

39
00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:40.600
Now follow the action through every hop,
and know where to look at each one.

40
00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:44.379
The middleware validates the
event, writes a provenance record

41
00:02:44.379 --> 00:02:47.587
in the agency's log store,
and sends the complete record.

42
00:02:47.587 --> 00:02:52.441
Because the analyst is still AD-mastered,
the agent updates AD and the next

43
00:02:52.441 --> 00:02:57.613
sync carries it up; attribute-change
triggers fire only after sync delivers it.

44
00:02:57.613 --> 00:03:03.062
Groups and units recompute, usually within
hours, but it can take more than a day.

45
00:03:03.062 --> 00:03:05.538
The record carries a
new division code too,

46
00:03:05.538 --> 00:03:10.488
so the analyst leaves the ledger groups and
the old division unit and joins accounts

47
00:03:10.488 --> 00:03:15.013
payable's groups and its unit, while the
analyst group and the persona stay put.

48
00:03:15.013 --> 00:03:20.112
The mover workflow swaps access packages
and starts a review of what's left.

49
00:03:20.112 --> 00:03:25.474
And at a database engine, access ends
only after tokens and sessions turn over.

50
00:03:25.474 --> 00:03:30.990
So latency is a design input: urgent
removal never waits for a rule.

51
00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:37.403
What must not change proves
as much as what does.

52
00:03:37.403 --> 00:03:39.666
The laptop's device groups stay put,

53
00:03:39.666 --> 00:03:43.102
because device rules can't
read the user's attributes.

54
00:03:43.102 --> 00:03:47.878
The service principal the analyst
sponsors still records the analyst's key,

55
00:03:47.878 --> 00:03:51.290
and a sponsor's own move
belongs to the non-person track.

56
00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:52.965
Nothing is assigned by hand.

57
00:03:52.965 --> 00:03:55.365
Then the proof, owned by the CISO.

58
00:03:55.365 --> 00:03:58.077
Run the drift scan; it
should find nothing.

59
00:03:58.077 --> 00:04:02.265
Rerun the kit with the earlier
snapshot and a fresh HR export.

60
00:04:02.265 --> 00:04:06.800
The membership check should show exactly
the expected changes and no others,

61
00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:11.051
the counts per organization code should
reconcile with HR, and the mover

62
00:04:11.051 --> 00:04:15.302
workflow's own run history should show
it ran for this user and what it did.

63
00:04:15.302 --> 00:04:18.642
The leaver-and-hire timeliness
check doesn't prove that,

64
00:04:18.642 --> 00:04:21.402
because it reads hire
and leave triggers only.

65
00:04:21.402 --> 00:04:23.981
Rollback is the simplest of any gate:

66
00:04:23.981 --> 00:04:29.052
reverse the personnel action in HR
and let the same path undo the access.

67
00:04:29.052 --> 00:04:31.560
Never fix the directory by hand.

68
00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:39.297
Here's what a failing run looks like,
so the first one isn't a surprise.

69
00:04:39.297 --> 00:04:42.422
These rows come from the
kit's own fictional tenant.

70
00:04:42.422 --> 00:04:46.026
One user group shrank by a
quarter since the last snapshot,

71
00:04:46.026 --> 00:04:49.930
and a device group doubled, so both
are findings; in a mover proof,

72
00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:53.309
a finding on a group the move
shouldn't touch means stop.

73
00:04:53.309 --> 00:04:54.885
Read the status carefully.

74
00:04:54.885 --> 00:04:56.735
Fail means at least one finding.

75
00:04:56.735 --> 00:04:58.410
Warn means something to review.

76
00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:03.310
Not applicable means nothing to check or
a missing input, and it's never a pass.

77
00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:06.435
Error means a read
failed, also never a pass.

78
00:05:06.435 --> 00:05:10.223
On the right is the evidence to
keep, joined on the person key.

79
00:05:10.223 --> 00:05:14.284
Entra ID keeps most of it for thirty
days at best, and the provenance

80
00:05:14.284 --> 00:05:18.710
record not at all, so route everything
out from the pilot's first day.

81
00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:26.497
Gate 8 begins by writing each
wave as an attribute query.

82
00:05:26.497 --> 00:05:30.757
Because waves are defined by
attribute values rather than OUs,

83
00:05:30.757 --> 00:05:35.098
each wave's scope is a query, and
its dynamic groups already exist.

84
00:05:35.098 --> 00:05:39.167
Each track picks members from its
own source: users by HR codes,

85
00:05:39.167 --> 00:05:44.353
devices by what provisioning writes on
them, servers by resource group and tag,

86
00:05:44.353 --> 00:05:48.023
and non-person identities by
their owning application.

87
00:05:48.023 --> 00:05:51.197
Start with the lowest-risk
region and a pilot division,

88
00:05:51.197 --> 00:05:55.217
then two or three divisions at a time,
each drift-clean before the next,

89
00:05:55.217 --> 00:05:58.461
with drift detection running
daily from the first wave.

90
00:05:58.461 --> 00:06:03.223
GPO links come off a migrated
OU only after seven clean days.

91
00:06:03.223 --> 00:06:06.922
The rule shown is the kit's
fixture form, with its guards.

92
00:06:06.922 --> 00:06:10.021
And in every wave, leaver
automation goes first,

93
00:06:10.021 --> 00:06:14.190
because revocation is where
the risk and the evidence are.

94
00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:20.800
Every wave passes the same attribute gate,

95
00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:25.703
straight from the Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 03's Appendix C.

96
00:06:25.703 --> 00:06:30.752
First, a validation report, signed
before the cutover is even scheduled.

97
00:06:30.752 --> 00:06:34.415
Second, a dry run on production
data with writes switched off,

98
00:06:34.415 --> 00:06:38.003
where every difference from
the directory gets explained.

99
00:06:38.003 --> 00:06:40.820
Third, the cutover, one
population at a time,

100
00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:44.441
with named records spot-checked
after the first cycle.

101
00:06:44.441 --> 00:06:50.653
Fourth, seven days in which every create,
change and disable traces to an HR event.

102
00:06:50.653 --> 00:06:53.302
Anything in the audit
log without a matching

103
00:06:53.302 --> 00:06:56.666
provisioning record is a
finding, and the clock restarts.

104
00:06:56.666 --> 00:06:58.448
No kit check covers that week;

105
00:06:58.448 --> 00:07:01.940
the provisioning logs joined
on the key are the evidence.

106
00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:05.290
Only then does the ticket path
for account creation close.

107
00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:07.339
The kit still runs on every wave,

108
00:07:07.339 --> 00:07:11.880
including a shadow comparison
for each group the wave replaces.

109
00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:20.105
As each population becomes
cloud-mastered, retarget its provisioning.

110
00:07:20.105 --> 00:07:24.901
Once its sync cycle completes, move
it out of the HR-to-AD scope and

111
00:07:24.901 --> 00:07:30.330
into the HR-to-Entra configuration,
leaving the AD leg dormant but intact.

112
00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:36.418
Microsoft lists entry checks: HR writes
Entra ID directly, no on-premises Exchange,

113
00:07:36.418 --> 00:07:41.342
no federation for those users, and
no apps that bind with passwords.

114
00:07:41.342 --> 00:07:43.355
Make those your per-population gate.

115
00:07:43.355 --> 00:07:49.014
One honest caveat: whether this
source-of-authority transfer works in GCC is still

116
00:07:49.014 --> 00:07:52.592
unverified, so give that
question an owner and a date,

117
00:07:52.592 --> 00:07:55.505
and plan on the AD-mastered
path by default.

118
00:07:55.505 --> 00:07:59.293
On the right is what still needs
AD, each with its response.

119
00:07:59.293 --> 00:08:02.760
And if a cutover goes wrong,
pause the provisioning job,

120
00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:08.060
which keeps its state, quarantine the
batch, and fall back to the prior process.

121
00:08:11.810 --> 00:08:16.438
At agency scale, Lifecycle Workflows'
limits bite in predictable ways,

122
00:08:16.438 --> 00:08:19.310
and the responses here
are design guidance.

123
00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:22.426
The tenant allows a hundred
workflows; one per tree,

124
00:08:22.426 --> 00:08:26.135
event and worker type would
already need a hundred and eight.

125
00:08:26.135 --> 00:08:29.323
So build a few general
workflows that branch on codes.

126
00:08:29.323 --> 00:08:34.133
An on-demand run skips the workflow's
own conditions and its execution limits,

127
00:08:34.133 --> 00:08:37.139
so run it on the wrong
people and nothing stops it;

128
00:08:37.139 --> 00:08:40.672
treat every on-demand run as
a change with a second person.

129
00:08:40.672 --> 00:08:44.853
Execution limits quarantine a mass
event until someone approves,

130
00:08:44.853 --> 00:08:49.261
so plan a reorganization as a rehearsed
change with an agreed release.

131
00:08:49.261 --> 00:08:53.051
The kit reports late HR data that
missed the three-day catch-up,

132
00:08:53.051 --> 00:08:58.090
workflows with no execution limit, and
a warning as you approach the ceiling.

133
00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:05.128
Retiring the OU tree waits
on the program twice.

134
00:09:05.128 --> 00:09:07.074
After the rollout is complete,

135
00:09:07.074 --> 00:09:11.590
remove the sync rule and scoping
filters that still read OU placement.

136
00:09:11.590 --> 00:09:16.397
After the OU-based groups retire,
remove the hand-made delegations,

137
00:09:16.397 --> 00:09:21.553
confirm no OU drives anything, and
archive the tree's final export in Git.

138
00:09:21.553 --> 00:09:24.639
Destructive steps need
approval one item at a time.

139
00:09:24.639 --> 00:09:30.227
To validate, a read-only script lists
every OU that still has a GPO linked to it;

140
00:09:30.227 --> 00:09:33.952
on a retired estate that
list is short and expected.

141
00:09:33.952 --> 00:09:39.689
Rollback means re-enabling, never re-creating:
turn the accounts and GPO links back

142
00:09:39.689 --> 00:09:45.340
on, return the population to the AD
provisioning scope, and reopen the ticket path.

143
00:09:45.340 --> 00:09:48.918
That's why you disable a link
instead of deleting the GPO.

144
00:09:48.918 --> 00:09:52.320
Deleting throws away the
one-command rollback.

145
00:09:56.070 --> 00:10:00.807
Gate 9 keeps it all true, and
without it everything slowly decays.

146
00:10:00.807 --> 00:10:03.632
It starts with the pilot,
not after cut-over.

147
00:10:03.632 --> 00:10:09.082
First, the CISO decides severity
and response times, decision D-A8.

148
00:10:09.082 --> 00:10:11.669
Then two comparisons run on a schedule.

149
00:10:11.669 --> 00:10:15.448
On the left, HR against the
directory: missing identities,

150
00:10:15.448 --> 00:10:20.029
stale access for people who've left,
orphan accounts, attribute drift,

151
00:10:20.029 --> 00:10:23.807
unauthorized writes, credential
drift and schema drift.

152
00:10:23.807 --> 00:10:27.920
The severities are the program's
defaults, not an external standard.

153
00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:32.617
On the right, the unit and role registry
against the tenant: unit rules that

154
00:10:32.617 --> 00:10:34.389
drifted, paused processing,

155
00:10:34.389 --> 00:10:38.470
role assignments nobody approved,
and restriction mismatches.

156
00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:42.370
Each track's own reconciliation
feeds the same findings queue.

157
00:10:42.370 --> 00:10:46.158
Expect a burst of high-severity
findings in the first month.

158
00:10:46.158 --> 00:10:50.080
That's the measurement finally
working, not the pipeline failing.

159
00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:57.955
The rule that makes drift close for
good is simple: fix at the source.

160
00:10:57.955 --> 00:11:01.350
Close each finding in the
HR record or the rule,

161
00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:05.817
never in the copy, because the
next cycle overwrites the copy.

162
00:11:05.817 --> 00:11:08.885
Access reviews don't
change dynamic membership,

163
00:11:08.885 --> 00:11:11.492
so review the rule and its inputs instead.

164
00:11:11.492 --> 00:11:13.616
Logs leave Entra ID from day one,

165
00:11:13.616 --> 00:11:17.292
because its retention is
short and never retroactive.

166
00:11:17.292 --> 00:11:22.116
Then keep a limits register and review it
with every design change: rule length,

167
00:11:22.116 --> 00:11:27.088
the dynamic group ceiling, the workflow
and extension budgets, restricted units,

168
00:11:27.088 --> 00:11:31.392
where dynamic units are available,
and the memberOf preview's end date.

169
00:11:31.392 --> 00:11:35.055
Every new workflow, unit
or rule spends a budget.

170
00:11:35.055 --> 00:11:38.540
Finally, run a full drift
cycle across the whole estate,

171
00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:43.300
closing findings at the source until
the nightly plan is zero-action.

172
00:11:47.050 --> 00:11:49.173
Gate 9's proof isn't a one-time run;

173
00:11:49.173 --> 00:11:52.438
it's a set of standing
checks on the drift cadence.

174
00:11:52.438 --> 00:11:55.423
For the HR track, the
uncorrelated accounts,

175
00:11:55.423 --> 00:11:58.326
the counts per code
and the duplicate keys.

176
00:11:58.326 --> 00:12:00.764
For groups and units, membership swings,

177
00:12:00.764 --> 00:12:04.163
paused processing and unit
rules against their groups.

178
00:12:04.163 --> 00:12:08.013
Each track also runs its own
standing checks in its own guide:

179
00:12:08.013 --> 00:12:13.019
devices missing their provisioning
attributes and stale devices for Intune,

180
00:12:13.019 --> 00:12:16.792
seven checks for Arc servers
from tags to role assignments,

181
00:12:16.792 --> 00:12:21.413
and owners, credentials and AI agent
sponsors for non-person identities.

182
00:12:21.413 --> 00:12:24.488
The commands differ by
track: delegated for HR,

183
00:12:24.488 --> 00:12:28.938
certificate-based for Intune, Reader
on the subscriptions for Arc.

184
00:12:28.938 --> 00:12:34.613
Behind them sits Book 01's verification
list, ending in zero drift findings.

185
00:12:34.613 --> 00:12:36.374
This gate has no rollback.

186
00:12:36.374 --> 00:12:38.080
It's the standing loop.

187
00:12:41.830 --> 00:12:45.618
Be clear about where the kits
stop, because an assessor will ask.

188
00:12:45.618 --> 00:12:50.284
Every gate now has a kit check, but most
leave a residual that no check reads,

189
00:12:50.284 --> 00:12:53.192
and the gate names the
evidence that stands in.

190
00:12:53.192 --> 00:12:58.318
For this part, Gate 7's checks cover the
membership change and the counts per code,

191
00:12:58.318 --> 00:13:03.055
but nothing proves the mover workflow
ran, so its own run history stands in.

192
00:13:03.055 --> 00:13:06.929
Gate 8 has its per-wave checks,
but nothing produces the seven-day

193
00:13:06.929 --> 00:13:11.168
sole-source report, so the provisioning
logs joined on the key stand in.

194
00:13:11.168 --> 00:13:15.346
Gate 9 has the HR, group and track
checks, but nothing compares role

195
00:13:15.346 --> 00:13:19.679
assignments with the registry, so
the registry diff in Git stands in.

196
00:13:19.679 --> 00:13:21.868
The earlier gates follow the same pattern.

197
00:13:21.868 --> 00:13:25.954
Sync, Conditional Access and the
PIV binding each have a check now,

198
00:13:25.954 --> 00:13:30.041
but the sync configuration, the
report-only results and whether a

199
00:13:30.041 --> 00:13:34.355
binding belongs to a current card
still come from a report or a record.

200
00:13:34.355 --> 00:13:36.680
Keep this table in the evidence packet.

201
00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:40.740
It shows which gates were proved
by code and which by a record.

202
00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:48.327
One honest slide about what the
overview made look simpler than it is.

203
00:13:48.327 --> 00:13:49.941
This is design guidance.

204
00:13:49.941 --> 00:13:52.728
First, a mover reaches more than groups.

205
00:13:52.728 --> 00:13:57.077
Access that came from a package or a
direct assignment doesn't leave when a

206
00:13:57.077 --> 00:14:01.427
rule stops matching, and losing a licensed
group can take a mailbox with it.

207
00:14:01.427 --> 00:14:04.303
That's why the review of
what's left is a control.

208
00:14:04.303 --> 00:14:07.165
Second, hybrid lasts longer than planned.

209
00:14:07.165 --> 00:14:12.241
Retiring AD means clearing named
dependencies, and the end state may be a dated

210
00:14:12.241 --> 00:14:17.552
Kerberos island, a program decision made
at the start and revisited after rollout.

211
00:14:17.552 --> 00:14:21.364
An island with no end date goes
to the authorizing official.

212
00:14:21.364 --> 00:14:23.727
Third, the numbers are proposals.

213
00:14:23.727 --> 00:14:28.441
Ninety-nine percent correlation is a
starting point, not a federal requirement.

214
00:14:28.441 --> 00:14:32.090
Treat every threshold as
a decision with an owner.

215
00:14:35.840 --> 00:14:38.702
To close, the decisions
and who makes them.

216
00:14:38.702 --> 00:14:42.856
The CISO signs off the mover
proof and owns drift severity

217
00:14:42.856 --> 00:14:46.416
and every finding, while HR
fixes every wrong value.

218
00:14:46.416 --> 00:14:49.128
The executive sponsor sets the wave plan,

219
00:14:49.128 --> 00:14:52.640
and identity engineering
calls each wave's go or no-go.

220
00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:55.506
A population's cutover is signed by HR,

221
00:14:55.506 --> 00:14:59.390
the identity owner, the ISSO
and the business owner.

222
00:14:59.390 --> 00:15:04.382
The GCC question needs a named owner
and a date, a reorganization's release

